f:["$","$L17",null,{"toc":[{"depth":2,"url":"#the-topology","title":"The topology"},{"depth":2,"url":"#what-a-worker-config-looks-like","title":"What a worker config looks like"},{"depth":2,"url":"#how-the-scheduler-picks-a-worker","title":"How the scheduler picks a worker"},{"depth":2,"url":"#scaling-the-pool","title":"Scaling the pool"},{"depth":2,"url":"#steps","title":"Steps"},{"depth":2,"url":"#the-shipped-example-and-where-its-readme-misleads","title":"The shipped example, and where its README misleads"},{"depth":2,"url":"#when-it-doesnt-work","title":"When it doesn't work"}],"full":"$undefined","children":[["$","$L18",null,{"children":"Run multiple workers"}],["$","$L19",null,{"children":"Add workers to a running Forge: how they share the CAS, how the scheduler matches them, and the two things about the shipped compose example that will mislead you."}],["$","$L1a",null,{"children":["\n",["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Who this is for:"}]," anyone with remote execution working against one worker\nwho now wants several. ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"What you'll have at the end:"}]," three workers pulling\nfrom one queue, and a clear idea of how to make it thirty. ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"Time:"}]," forty\nminutes."]}],"\n",["$","div",null,{"data-prerequisites":true,"className":"my-6 rounded-xl border border-border bg-surface-elevated px-5 py-3","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"my-0 font-mono text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.12em] text-brand","children":"Before you start"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"mt-1 text-sm text-muted-foreground [&>p]:my-0","children":["$","p",null,{"children":["A working scheduler and at least one worker. See ",["$","$L1b",null,{"href":"/remote-execution","children":"Remote execution"}],"."]}]}]]}],"\n",["$","p",null,{"children":"One worker is a demonstration. The reason to add more is that the scheduler\nalready assumes them: it holds a queue, and every worker that connects to the\nworker API announces what it can run and starts taking work off that queue.\nNothing about adding the second worker is different from adding the tenth."}],"\n",["$","p",null,{"children":"What does need thought is the part that goes wrong most often: workers do not\nshare a disk. They share the CAS, and they share it over gRPC."}],"\n",["$","$L1c",null,{"as":"h2","id":"the-topology","children":"The topology"}],"\n",["$","p",null,{"children":"Three roles, three kinds of process, on one network:"}],"\n",["$","$L1d",null,{"children":"flowchart LR\nC[\"Bazel client\"] -->|\":50051 CAS + AC\"| CAS[\"CAS server\"]\nC -->|\":50052 execution\"| S[\"Scheduler\"]\nS -->|\"grpc store\"| CAS\nW1[\"worker-1\"] -->|\":50061 worker API\"| S\nW2[\"worker-2\"] -->|\":50061 worker API\"| S\nW3[\"worker-3\"] -->|\":50061 worker API\"| S\nW1 -->|\"grpc store\"| CAS\nW2 -->|\"grpc store\"| CAS\nW3 -->|\"grpc store\"| CAS"}],"\n",["$","p",null,{"children":"The CAS server owns the bytes. The scheduler owns the queue. Workers own\nnothing durable. They are the disposable tier, which is exactly why they are\nthe tier you scale."}],"\n",["$","p",null,{"children":["Each worker keeps a local filesystem cache in front of the shared CAS so that\nan input it has fetched once does not cross the network again. That is a\n",["$","code",null,{"children":"fast_slow"}]," store whose ",["$","code",null,{"children":"slow"}]," half is a ",["$","code",null,{"children":"grpc"}]," store pointing at the CAS\nserver. ",["$","$L1b",null,{"href":"/how-to/stores/compose-stores","children":"Compose stores"}]," covers the wrapper in\ngeneral; this is its most load-bearing use."]}],"\n",["$","a",null,{"href":"https://github.com/toxicwind/forge/blob/v1.6.5/forge-config/src/cas_server.rs","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","data-source-link":"forge-config/src/cas_server.rs","data-source-ref":"v1.6.5","title":"forge-config/src/cas_server.rs at v1.6.5","className":"inline-flex items-center gap-1 rounded-md border 